s is done for  a few more generations what will be the  result the progeny at the end will have  these genes conserved but notice  something interesting as we keep  crossing the individuals in the same  family the chances of obtaining the  homozygous form is enhanced this process  of crossing individuals from the same  family  called inbreeding and offsprings  obtained after in reading several  generations are called pure lines one of  the advantages of using pure lines while  beginning the experiment is to have an  assurance that the organism is  homozygous even for the dominant trait  now let's focus back on the cross we  were about to carry out this time let's  consider the character stem height in  the pea plant here we take a tall and a  dwarf plant both these are pure lines on  crossing the parental generation we get  these offsprings in the f1 generation  now what will happen when we cross these  two plants from the f1 generation it  will give us these results take a moment  to go through this so what we get is  three tall plants and one dwarf plant  isn't this the same ratio that Mendel  obtained when flower color was  considered as the character yes it is he  had obtained three purple and one white  flower in the f2 generation similarly  here we get three tall and one Dwarfs  plant now that we've had a good idea  about crossing let me introduce two new  terms to you trust me they are very  simple to understand the two terms are  phenotypic ratio and genotypic ratio in  this case the phenotypic ratio is three  to one that is three tall plants and one  dwarf plant and the genotypic ratio is  one to two to one as we can see one  plant is homozygous for the tall trait  these two are heterozygous and this one  is homozygous for the recessive trait  dwarf any idea what these terms mean  let's understand them first and then  proceed with understanding the ratios  the features that can be seen or  observed by us externally are called  phenotypes  fino is a prefix t